--- body ---
## Context

Split out of ticket 0793, which had it as action 6. It does not belong there:
0793 asks one question — is the native worker slow or wedged — and this question
is orthogonal to it and carries a hard dependency 0793 does not. Leaving it in
would have held 0793 open behind a fixture import that has not started.

`plugins/sdt-sitter/scheduler.js` only relays the number `Zotero.SDT.ensure()`
hands its `onProgress` callback. Whether that number tracks pages, bytes,
extracted characters or something else is unread on this side, and it changes
what a plateau means. If progress counts pages while the work per page varies by
orders of magnitude, a bar advancing evenly says nothing about work remaining,
and a stall at 90 % on a document with heavy tail pages is expected rather than
pathological. If it tracks text, an even climb is a real rate and a plateau is a
real anomaly.

This is worth settling on its own terms, whatever 0793 concludes. It decides
whether the progress number can ever carry a prediction, and it is the
precondition for any "N % done" wording the UI might want.

## Dependency

**Blocked by 0794**, which imports the bench: `gri_33125001118088`, the 1762
*Recueil de planches*, 758 pages whose per-page character counts are already
measured — two modes a factor of 200 apart, at 31 and 6 639 characters, with an
overall median of 25 and 61 % of pages under 50 characters, structured in blocks
rather than alternating.
Nothing currently in the fixture cache has that shape, which is exactly why this
ticket cannot run before 0794 lands.

`Blocked-by` names 0794 and not 0793: the dependency is the fixture, not the
stall investigation.

## Relevant files

- `bench/fixtures/` and the menagerie recipe — where 0794 lands the document.
- `plugins/sdt-sitter/scheduler.js` — the `onProgress` relay; read, not changed.
- `bench/sdt_stall_probe.py` — `analyse_heartbeats()` already parses the journal
  and reports ticks; the tick series it reads is the input to this measurement.
- `verification/incidents/0793-2026-09-15-ar6-stall.json` — the trace whose
  interpretation this ticket sharpens.

## Actions

1. With 0794's plates volume imported, run it through `ensure()` in an
   instrumented session and capture the full `progress` record series with
   timestamps.
2. Map each progress tick onto the page range it must correspond to under each
   hypothesis, using the per-page character counts 0794 records.
3. Compare: ticks even across the plate run (where pages are near-empty) means
   the number counts **pages**; ticks clustered on the explication pages means it
   counts **text**. A third shape — neither — is a real outcome and is reported
   as such rather than forced into one of the two.
4. Record what this implies for reading a plateau, and feed it back into 0793's
   write-up if 0793 is still open.

## Test

The control is the *text* volume 0794 also names — the same Encyclopédie in a
unimodal edition, 97 % of pages between 3 000 and 6 461 characters. Under either
hypothesis its ticks are even, so it cannot discriminate; it is there to show
that the instrumentation produces a readable tick series at all on a document of
that size, before a null on the plates volume is allowed to mean anything.

## Verification

- [ ] Full progress-tick series captured for the plates volume, with timestamps
- [ ] Per-page character counts from 0794 aligned against the tick positions
- [ ] Control run on the unimodal text volume in the same session
- [ ] The answer stated as pages / text / neither, with the alignment as evidence

## Invariants

- No change to `plugins/sdt-sitter/`. This ticket measures; it does not fix.
- `make check` passes.

## Exit criteria

- A recorded answer to "what does the progress number count", with the tick
  alignment that supports it, in `verification/`.
- A one-line statement of what a plateau in that number does and does not imply,
  cited from 0793 (or from its successor if 0793 has closed).
